1
00:00:11,201 --> 00:00:11,942
Good evening.

2
00:00:12,163 --> 00:00:14,086
I trust you had a fruitful day.

3
00:00:14,266 --> 00:00:17,931
I do not say happy, though I hope, of course, it has been a very happy day.

4
00:00:18,974 --> 00:00:35,859
Namo tassa bhagavato arahato sambha sambhu dasa Namo tassa bhagavato arahato sambha sambhu dasa

5
00:00:37,173 --> 00:00:47,246
Namo tassa bhagavato arahato samma sambuddhassa.

6
00:00:48,488 --> 00:00:52,234
Homage to the Buddha, the blessed, noble and fully self-awakened one.

7
00:01:01,487 --> 00:01:02,588
I don't think it's copied.

8
00:01:04,490 --> 00:01:06,453
So, okay, this evening,

9
00:01:07,548 --> 00:01:10,712
I want to do an overview of two things.

10
00:01:10,813 --> 00:01:16,481
First of all, the discourse that we covered last night and the earlier discourse about the self.

11
00:01:17,143 --> 00:01:21,090
And I just want to go through this schema.

12
00:01:22,572 --> 00:01:28,662
Hopefully we can see how it all fits together and we'll take it from there.

13
00:01:30,667 --> 00:01:34,772
So the Buddha says that we suffer from a delusion, right?

14
00:01:34,813 --> 00:01:39,281
That's one of the basic, that's the platform upon which everything goes wrong.

15
00:01:39,760 --> 00:01:41,143
Some form of delusion.

16
00:01:41,164 --> 00:01:43,927
And this delusion manifests as a self.

17
00:01:45,590 --> 00:01:48,334
The sense of self, the view of self, right?

18
00:01:49,036 --> 00:01:57,989
Now, when we think about the view of self, we're talking about how I understand myself to be or how people understand themselves to be.

19
00:01:58,695 --> 00:02:06,165
So the first one is that this is what we did when we did right at the beginning of this series.

20
00:02:07,826 --> 00:02:13,394
There are people who say that I exist and I will continue to exist.

21
00:02:13,413 --> 00:02:18,840
So that's an eternalist idea that this sense of self will continue forever.

22
00:02:19,822 --> 00:02:23,605
And of course, there are those materialists who say that's a load of rubbish.

23
00:02:23,926 --> 00:02:25,467
When you die, that's the end of it.

24
00:02:25,669 --> 00:02:26,830
So that's the first two.

25
00:02:27,383 --> 00:02:31,147
Now, the next three are to do with this sense of self-awareness.

26
00:02:32,631 --> 00:02:37,638
So the first one is that the self-awareness and what I'm observing is all me.

27
00:02:38,299 --> 00:02:41,182
So it's another eternalist view, you might say.

28
00:02:41,824 --> 00:02:45,748
Everything that I'm aware of and I'm aware of myself is all me.

29
00:02:48,193 --> 00:02:49,495
Then there's a separation.

30
00:02:49,555 --> 00:02:55,203
Some people feel that, well, I am the self-awareness, but I'm not what I'm aware of.

31
00:02:55,522 --> 00:02:57,306
The self-awareness is my spirit.

32
00:02:58,027 --> 00:03:05,594
So that's still an eternalist thing, but at least it doesn't include everything that we experience.

33
00:03:05,615 --> 00:03:08,197
It's more spiritual.

34
00:03:09,959 --> 00:03:13,901
And some say the observed is real, but not the self-awareness.

35
00:03:13,961 --> 00:03:22,169
So that's, of course, a materialist view where the sense of self is an emergent property from the physical brain.

36
00:03:23,751 --> 00:03:26,212
And that, of course, is an annihilationist point of view.

37
00:03:28,200 --> 00:03:34,027
And the final one is everything I experienced myself to be, et cetera, et cetera, is eternal, right?

38
00:03:34,048 --> 00:03:34,848
It's always there.

39
00:03:35,349 --> 00:03:36,912
I can always go back to it.

40
00:03:37,012 --> 00:03:37,913
I can always be it.

41
00:03:38,253 --> 00:03:40,736
So that's a really full blown eternalist view.

42
00:03:40,776 --> 00:03:45,342
In other words, when I'm, when I die, I will be exactly like me.

43
00:03:45,842 --> 00:03:52,031
And as you know, some people believe that they will be, uh, come back to the earth and with the renewed body.

44
00:03:52,532 --> 00:03:52,891
Okay.

45
00:03:53,513 --> 00:03:56,056
So there are all these views of a self.

46
00:03:57,056 --> 00:04:10,512
And this comes under, first of all, the delusion, but it also comes under this trilogy of wrong view, attachment and conceit.

47
00:04:10,913 --> 00:04:13,215
It manifests as conceit, right?

48
00:04:13,596 --> 00:04:18,942
Which is some sort of relationship always that we have with the other.

49
00:04:18,961 --> 00:04:22,625
So I'm better than, I'm not as good as,

50
00:04:23,011 --> 00:04:24,752
and I'm equal to, right?

51
00:04:24,773 --> 00:04:28,697
Now, remember that equal to is a bit of a, is also a conceit.

52
00:04:28,817 --> 00:04:29,678
It sounds humble.

53
00:04:29,879 --> 00:04:30,860
Oh, I'm equal to you.

54
00:04:30,940 --> 00:04:41,774
But remember, you've created a little island of a group which compares itself to another group, which is, of course, superior, and another group which is infinite.

55
00:04:43,216 --> 00:04:45,038
Okay, so that's the basis of the self.

56
00:04:46,321 --> 00:04:49,845
Now, this self now begins to perceive.

57
00:04:50,584 --> 00:04:56,497
And here, when it perceives, this original delusion distorts the perception.

58
00:04:56,576 --> 00:04:59,783
In other words, it's not seeing things as they are.

59
00:04:59,802 --> 00:05:03,129
It's always seeing things through this level of delusion.

60
00:05:04,892 --> 00:05:12,507
So, first of all, that we tend to see permanent, at least semi-permanent, what is actually fundamentally impermanent.

61
00:05:14,362 --> 00:05:18,928
So we tend to presume that things will stay the same.

62
00:05:19,088 --> 00:05:20,190
It's just one of those things.

63
00:05:20,250 --> 00:05:26,360
That's when things change, especially radically, we're always in a state of shock.

64
00:05:27,180 --> 00:05:32,208
So that's because beneath it all, we presume that, you know, life will just carry on much the same.

65
00:05:34,110 --> 00:05:36,814
We mistake the painful for the pleasant.

66
00:05:36,855 --> 00:05:38,697
So that's to do with indulgence.

67
00:05:39,067 --> 00:05:45,437
So when we're indulging ourselves, it's great, but we don't see the consequences of it.

68
00:05:45,456 --> 00:05:50,964
So we mistake it for something that's really pleasing when in fact it's got a sting in the tail.

69
00:05:55,593 --> 00:05:57,334
And the not self is self.

70
00:05:57,435 --> 00:06:02,062
So this is the idea that something in me is completely isolated.

71
00:06:02,642 --> 00:06:05,648
It's a whole entire unto itself.

72
00:06:05,947 --> 00:06:10,213
But when, of course, I look into it, I find it's all made up of bits and pieces.

73
00:06:11,535 --> 00:06:22,209
And the final one is to mistake what is ultimately ugly or foul or not pleasant for what is pleasant.

74
00:06:22,649 --> 00:06:27,175
And again, we're talking about relationship to human beings, etc.

75
00:06:27,656 --> 00:06:31,281
So as we say, beauty is only skin deep.

76
00:06:34,146 --> 00:06:40,315
So those are the distortions of perception that arise with the self, right?

77
00:06:40,355 --> 00:06:43,620
The perversions, as they're called, perversions, vipalasa.

78
00:06:44,922 --> 00:06:52,533
Now, running alongside this are our feelings, our emotions, our moods, right?

79
00:06:52,552 --> 00:06:57,339
And I'm not really talking here about the physical ones, just the heart ones.

80
00:06:58,540 --> 00:07:00,423
Now, the heart isn't logical.

81
00:07:00,757 --> 00:07:09,701
When we say in the evening, when we say, may all beings be free of physical pain, we know all beings are not going to be free of physical pain, but it's coming from the heart.

82
00:07:09,721 --> 00:07:11,365
The heart isn't rational.

83
00:07:11,846 --> 00:07:15,375
It's just an emotional opening, as it were.

84
00:07:17,430 --> 00:07:26,247
But of course it can distort reason, it can either support reason or distort it.

85
00:07:26,668 --> 00:07:39,675
So as you can see with these fanatics, this grasping, this tanha, this holding on to their anger and their hatred can cause tremendous suffering for others.

86
00:07:40,346 --> 00:07:42,990
On the other hand, it can be very supportive.

87
00:07:43,370 --> 00:07:49,036
So anger, say, about the climate crisis can be turned into very positive action.

88
00:07:49,416 --> 00:07:50,317
It moves you.

89
00:07:50,637 --> 00:07:52,821
It's a force that can push you there.

90
00:07:53,281 --> 00:07:58,086
But of course, it's got to move from being anger to being active compassion.

91
00:07:58,526 --> 00:08:00,007
And that's the problem.

92
00:08:01,610 --> 00:08:06,134
So it's the distinction between being reactive

93
00:08:06,317 --> 00:08:33,539
and um responsive right so when we find ourselves just reacting reacting we know we're coming from a delusive position the heart that is i'm talking about and when we're actually responding out of love compassion joy uh then that's coming out more out of wisdom even though we may be coming from a wiser position remember this underlying all this delusion of self

94
00:08:36,288 --> 00:08:39,052
So that's the second one, that's the grasping.

95
00:08:39,673 --> 00:08:45,120
Now, these perceptions that we've talked about, when we perceive something, they grow into concepts.

96
00:08:45,159 --> 00:08:48,403
And that's what we were discussing yesterday, mainly.

97
00:08:49,846 --> 00:08:57,856
And the first example that he gave is, of course, the four great elements, which is how we experience the body.

98
00:08:58,461 --> 00:09:06,250
So the body becomes an identity, the body as me, okay?

99
00:09:06,309 --> 00:09:08,532
There's your first concept, right?

100
00:09:08,552 --> 00:09:11,394
So you have the body and it's me.

101
00:09:12,216 --> 00:09:16,279
And then you have this business of I am in the body, right?

102
00:09:16,299 --> 00:09:18,101
There's me separate from the body.

103
00:09:18,121 --> 00:09:24,687
So the first part that we talked about, the self, is about how the self experiences itself.

104
00:09:24,707 --> 00:09:28,231
This is how it experiences other things,

105
00:09:28,414 --> 00:09:47,283
the things that it experiences and the next one is I am NOT the body right there's a separation and the final one is I'm I own the body so I am the body I am in the body I'm not the body and I own the body

106
00:09:47,533 --> 00:09:49,515
So there's your concepts.

107
00:09:49,535 --> 00:09:53,681
And this translates into everything that we experience.

108
00:09:54,240 --> 00:09:59,527
So you might not be with objects, you own them.

109
00:09:59,547 --> 00:10:00,889
You say, well, this is mine.

110
00:10:01,288 --> 00:10:07,096
And then that creates a relationship of possession so that when you lose it, there's suffering.

111
00:10:07,115 --> 00:10:16,326
When we tell ourselves that we can only actually use an object, the loss of it doesn't hit the heart.

112
00:10:16,643 --> 00:10:20,807
And if somebody steals it, well, you offer it to them, even if it's your mobile.

113
00:10:20,826 --> 00:10:21,788
That's very difficult, I know.

114
00:10:23,710 --> 00:10:34,900
Now, this conceiving, of course, moves with the emotional life, moves into what's called sanya-sankara, sanya-sanya-papancha.

115
00:10:35,140 --> 00:10:42,447
So that means that both when these feelings, when these emotional mental states

116
00:10:42,884 --> 00:10:52,139
join, support a concept, an idea, then of course the mind begins to create.

117
00:10:53,120 --> 00:10:57,287
And if it's based on something unwholesome, then it won't be very good.

118
00:10:57,346 --> 00:11:01,013
But if it's based on something wholesome, at least something wholesome will come of it.

119
00:11:03,056 --> 00:11:08,283
So normally speaking, this is a bit of a downer that they have this proliferation.

120
00:11:08,533 --> 00:11:11,398
because it's presumed that it's always coming from the self.

121
00:11:11,899 --> 00:11:12,538
And that's true.

122
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:29,182
But the effect of the selfishness of the self can be attenuated by the practice of renunciation, the hatred by the practice of love, and the cruelty by the practice of compassion, and so on and so forth.

123
00:11:30,102 --> 00:11:35,470
All these vices that we find in ourselves, these horrible things that we find in ourselves,

124
00:11:35,837 --> 00:11:46,134
They can be changed towards their opposite by us, by being aware of what we're doing and putting the right intention into our hearts.

125
00:11:46,654 --> 00:11:51,062
So although it's proliferation, the proliferation can be a benefit.

126
00:11:51,442 --> 00:11:53,947
Hence, you know, we do things that are good.

127
00:11:54,267 --> 00:11:58,333
We create things that are good for ourselves, for others, et cetera.

128
00:11:59,836 --> 00:12:02,841
So there we have that process, right?

129
00:12:03,344 --> 00:12:20,504
from this delusive moment where we believe ourselves to be this psycho-physical organism in various ways, we perceive things in a slightly distorted way and have a reaction to them in the heart in a slightly distorted way.

130
00:12:21,125 --> 00:12:31,258
These two conjoin together to create, sorry, the perceptions then move into a more conceptual idea with ideas, philosophies, etc.

131
00:12:31,643 --> 00:12:36,748
And when that joins with the heart, you have this proliferation.

132
00:12:36,768 --> 00:12:38,610
And that's what happens when you're sitting here.

133
00:12:38,971 --> 00:12:48,961
See, if you're sitting in your meditation and off you go, and you're, you're finally found the answer to, you know, um, climate crisis, and this is what you're going to do.

134
00:12:48,980 --> 00:12:50,101
And you're going to save the world.

135
00:12:50,121 --> 00:12:54,525
Then this is, this is the proliferation, right?

136
00:12:54,547 --> 00:12:59,912
If you're studying the Dharma and you're actually thinking seriously about it, then of course,

137
00:13:00,128 --> 00:13:28,296
proliferation is a benefit benefit to yourself and others with a bit of luck so that's a sort of hopefully you've got the flow of that right from delusion the continual selfing and included in this by the way our dependent origination the five candors uh uh the six sense bases and what comes out of those you can see how enormously complicated this whole thing is

138
00:13:29,322 --> 00:13:35,947
So from the sense of self, we have mistaken perceptions that move into mistaken concepts.

139
00:13:35,967 --> 00:13:51,621
And on the other side, on the heart side, we have these reactions which are based on the self and therefore tend to be, if they're based on the self, they tend to be unwholesome, holding onto things, acquisitiveness, hatred, et cetera, et cetera.

140
00:13:52,001 --> 00:13:57,206
And when these two come together, then that's how we behave.

141
00:13:57,226 --> 00:13:59,328
So being aware of this,

142
00:13:59,544 --> 00:14:12,625
we pull back on it, we try not to... we question our understandings and we don't allow the heart to push us forward so much with its negativities.

143
00:14:13,024 --> 00:14:17,272
Slowly everything begins to purify, we become more clear about things.

144
00:14:19,254 --> 00:14:23,640
And that's the process of awakening, awakening to the way things really are.

145
00:14:24,842 --> 00:14:28,168
So I hope that gives you some idea

146
00:14:28,451 --> 00:14:47,371
of the complexity of the buddha's teachings now you can ask yourself of course is this absolutely necessary i mean do we have to know this stuff and uh the answer is no no this is uh this is this it is for some people it's necessary they're very intellectual they like to go into things

147
00:14:47,825 --> 00:14:58,716
And for people who are not so intellectual, at least it gives them that confidence that comes from knowing that this system has really been understood extremely deeply by the Buddha.

148
00:14:59,236 --> 00:15:02,341
And I've not done it any justice whatsoever.

149
00:15:02,660 --> 00:15:08,386
Remember Bhikkhu Bodhi's written a book on this topic.

150
00:15:08,407 --> 00:15:11,250
So then the end of the discourse now.

151
00:15:12,547 --> 00:15:40,865
um is about uh you know the person who finally who finally sheds all this completely so the first one is the arahat the one who's completely free of tanks his old tanks the oozings have been destroyed who has lived the holy life done what had to be done laid down the burden oh how wonderful laid down the burden reached the true goal destroyed the fetters

152
00:15:41,032 --> 00:16:06,735
of being the chains of becoming and is completely liberated through final knowledge and directly he knows the earth is earth the body is the body etc etc and having directly known this he does not conceive he does not wrongly conceive uh he has no wrong conception shall we say why because he's fully understood it okay

153
00:16:09,212 --> 00:16:20,624
And the same is said again, that he's come to this position because he is free of lust through the destruction of lust, right?

154
00:16:21,083 --> 00:16:33,196
So in other words, there's no, actually, the translation here is of raga, which lust, of course, has that meaning of sexuality about it.

155
00:16:33,216 --> 00:16:37,379
But this is more like passion, indulgent passion, passionate indulgence.

156
00:16:38,203 --> 00:16:46,812
but it stands really for any craving, even the slightest craving.

157
00:16:46,831 --> 00:16:54,139
So he's destroyed it because the actual core problem of craving has been completely destroyed.

158
00:16:56,380 --> 00:17:04,509
And that's the same with hatred because he is free from the hate through the destruction of hatred.

159
00:17:04,528 --> 00:17:07,991
Remember this again, although it's translated as hatred,

160
00:17:08,173 --> 00:17:31,718
aversion i always think is a better word because it also includes the idea of fear because at the basis of everything is fear right it's the fear of uh of losing one's life the fear that comes from knowing the instability of the world that we have this idea of acquiring things to make ourselves feel safe

161
00:17:32,238 --> 00:17:55,950
and pushing away getting rid of anything which is is taking away that sense of safety and of course finally he's free of that delusion that fundamental delusion as to the self uh through the destruction of delusion itself that's the last thing to go that delusion that ignorance that not knowing

162
00:17:58,359 --> 00:18:03,964
So that's why a person is fully liberated.

163
00:18:04,965 --> 00:18:16,478
Now the Tathagata, which is the way the Buddha talked about himself, pointing to himself, he is also accomplished and fully enlightened, directly knows all this stuff.

164
00:18:17,578 --> 00:18:25,346
And he does not mistake all these conceptions because the Tathagata has fully understood it to the end.

165
00:18:25,486 --> 00:18:28,249
In other words, as deep as you can go,

166
00:18:28,701 --> 00:18:36,830
concerning our condition, the Buddha has understood that.

167
00:18:36,851 --> 00:18:42,257
And he's done the same because he has understood the indulgence is the root of suffering.

168
00:18:42,737 --> 00:18:44,239
That's the tanha, the desire.

169
00:18:45,039 --> 00:18:54,150
And that with this as a condition, there is birth and that whatever has come to become, there is aging and death.

170
00:18:55,131 --> 00:18:57,814
Therefore, through the complete destruction,

171
00:18:58,367 --> 00:19:10,463
fading away, cessation, giving up and relinquishing of those cravings that the Tathagata has awakened to the supreme full enlightenment, I say.

172
00:19:10,483 --> 00:19:16,290
So he's really stressing the fact that all that craving is completely gone in him.

173
00:19:16,672 --> 00:19:20,696
Not even traces of it are left in the mind, heart mind.

174
00:19:23,696 --> 00:19:37,174
And again, therefore, through the complete destruction, fading away, cessation, giving up and relinquishment of all those cravings that a tiger that has wakened to the supreme full enlightenment, I say.

175
00:19:38,096 --> 00:19:41,800
So basically, he's the master.

176
00:19:41,820 --> 00:19:43,123
He's the one who knows.

177
00:19:45,652 --> 00:19:54,742
And of course, he's saying this to give them confidence because the ending of this discourse is quite rare.

178
00:19:54,803 --> 00:20:02,352
This is what the Blessed One said, but the Bhikkhus did not delight in the Blessed One's words.

179
00:20:02,372 --> 00:20:03,173
Now, there we are.

180
00:20:03,532 --> 00:20:05,375
They weren't happy at all with what he said.

181
00:20:06,115 --> 00:20:11,962
And it is said that he then later gave a discourse where he expresses

182
00:20:12,247 --> 00:20:17,093
that this stuff he knows for himself is a direct experience.

183
00:20:17,113 --> 00:20:24,482
And this gives these people, these listeners, enough confidence to become fully liberated.

184
00:20:25,203 --> 00:20:28,367
But let's just see why they weren't happy.

185
00:20:28,469 --> 00:20:32,314
Let's see what the commentaries say.

